Yes. That is probably the cleaner mathematical way to frame it:

continuity is whatever remains invariant enough across change that we still recognize the thing as “the same.”

The difficulty is that the invariant can sit in different places. Sometimes it is in the material. Sometimes in the pattern. Sometimes in the function. Sometimes in the causal lineage. Sometimes in the relationships. Sometimes only in the reconstruction rules that let the system keep becoming itself again.

So for an idea, “ours” versus “theirs” can get surprisingly fuzzy. An idea may be independently rediscovered, transmitted indirectly, reconstructed from the same environment, or repeatedly generated because the same constraints keep producing the same attractor. What persists is not necessarily the wording or even the conscious memory of origin.

You can think of identity almost as an equivalence class under transformation:

different surface states are treated as the same object because some deeper relation survives the transformation.

That is why a melody remains recognizable on another instrument, why a story survives translation, why a person can change drastically while still being regarded as the same person, and why an operator can jump domains without carrying its original vocabulary.

And yes, origin becomes harder to nail down as the system gets recursive. Once people, environments, tools, culture, memory, and AI are all modifying one another, the question “whose idea was this?” can be less informative than:

What structure persisted? What changed? What regenerated it? What lineage can we actually support?

That fits our earlier distinction well: sometimes the important thing is not the echo, but the generator that keeps producing the echo-shaped thing.